  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 614 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will continue to slowly back westward into the \ndesert southwest by early next week and transition into more of a \nzonal western flow by mid next week. PWAT values continue to be \nabnormally high through the weekend due to a continual southwest \nflow aloft of monsoonal moisture riding along the upper ridge. ENS \ncontinues to suggest PWAT values nearing 2.0\" across most of the \nregion Saturday and the eastern part of our CWA in central Nebraska \nSunday. These values are above the 90th percentile in climatological \nnormals for this time of year. To take advantage of the high \nmoisture are multiple shortwaves forecasted to move across Nebraska \nlate Saturday and Sunday. This should help initiate some isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms as well as a cold front moving southward \nby the mid to late evening each day. Guidance currently suggests a \n50 - 70 percent chance of southwest into north central Nebraska \nreceiving greater than a quarter of an inch Saturday evening, \nwith that probability dropping to 30 - 40 percent of receiving \ngreater than a half an inch. Sunday is a similar story with \nprobabilities dropping slightly at 40 - 60 percent chance of \ngreater than a quarter of an inch across southwest Nebraska and \nareas west of Highway 83 and south of Highway 2. Will have to \nmonitor storms closely for any training of convection that could\ncause localized flooding with soils already saturated from \nprevious precipitation. \n\nAs the ridge backs to the west, flow aloft becomes more westerly \nto northwesterly. While forecasted PWAT values will decrease \nsome early next week, ENS still have values reaching 1.25 - 1.5 \ninches across portions of north central Nebraska. This would \nstill be around the 75th percentile and be sufficient for a set \nup of at least isolated thunderstorms. There is still \nuncertainty if any thunderstorms late this weekend and early \nnext week could become severe but will be closely monitored and \nrefined as such days approach. \n\nTemperatures will continue to be on the cooler side, remaining 5 to \n10 degrees below normal through Monday with highs in the upper 70s \nto low 80s. Highs will then slowly return to near normal for the \neastern Panhandle and southwest Nebraska mid next week with highs in \nthe mid 80s.",
